---
import StellarCharacterSketch from "../../../components/studies/StellarCharacterSketch.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "stellar-characters-2026-11-11",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Recognising what lies ahead</h2>
    <p>
      A pilot approaching the established standard Sun can collect energy while
      contending with gravitational pull, exposure and lethal contact. A
      different stellar nature should invite a different reading of the
      encounter: what is tempting, what threatens, and whether to approach, wait
      or leave. Colour, surface structure and activity around the body must make
      that character visible together, within the game’s high-contrast
      procedural art direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      The subject is the stellar encounter’s visible identity and the approach
      it suggests. Candidates must differ in nature, rather than merely resize
      or recolour the Sun. A white giant, a blue dwarf and a violently active
      star are research prompts, not established categories or a selected
      roster. Each candidate needs a scientific basis connected to a proposed
      pilot experience and a concrete visual alternative. Observed phenomena,
      speculative possibilities and deliberate arcade abstractions must be
      distinguished. The tension is between an unfamiliar stellar character and
      cues a pilot can understand before committing to danger.
    </p>
    <p>
      The standard Sun and current ship handling remain the reference. Candidate
      appearances, activity and encounter opportunities remain open; new
      mechanics are hypotheses until investigated. Visual comparisons must
      precede roster selection and numerical tuning. Close views establish
      surface character; flight-scale views and sequences establish what remains
      legible and how activity warns. These examples can test communication, but
      cannot establish survival balance. Generation, progression and changes to
      ship handling are adjacent questions outside this cycle. This cycle
      supersedes the earlier stellar-encounters inquiry for stellar identity;
      its open registry status does not make it a dependency or a second owner
      of this answer. Earlier spatial observations are limited evidence; its
      roster and delivery scope have no authority here.
    </p>
    <p class="question-check">
      Question test · 11 November 2026: One subject — Pass after correction and
      recheck; Grounded — Pass; Consequence — Pass.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/stellar-characters-2026-11-11"
        >Full review and ownership finding</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="021">02.1 / Wind, eruption, wreckage, sweep</h2>
    <p>
      Four encounter hypotheses beside the unchanged Sun. Each plate has a close
      view and three smaller encounter frames, read left to right. The Wasp
      stays in place so the changing surroundings carry the comparison. These
      are procedural concept storyboards, not simulated approaches or proposed
      field boundaries. Body sizes are pictorial; the close views are enlarged
      independently. Neither sizes nor intervals specify game tuning.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Sun and Wasp use their production drawing functions. Candidate artwork
      is study-local. All sequences are static, deterministic and equally
      inspectable without animation. Surface detail may suggest a nature at
      close range; the smaller frames test which surrounding cues could carry it
      in flight. The interaction-led direction is retained below; no exact
      artwork, mechanic, numerical profile or complete roster is selected.
    </p>
    <h3>Reference / The steady bargain</h3>
    <StellarCharacterSketch
      client:load
      kind="sun"
      label="Production standard Sun: flat amber body and three decorative expanding rings, with the same Wasp in three fixed compositions"
      moments={["01 / STEADY", "02 / STEADY", "03 / STEADY"]}
    />
    <p class="caption">
      Unchanged G artwork at illustrative zooms, sampled at 700, 1150 and 1600
      ms. Its rings are decorative, not warnings of timed damage. Collection,
      pull, exposure and contact retain the
      <a href="/lab/entities/star">standard Sun’s rules</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="candidate" id="white-supergiant">
    <p class="candidate-id">A / WHITE SUPERGIANT · OBSERVED NATURE</p>
    <h3>A luminous sea that pours outward</h3>
    <StellarCharacterSketch
      client:load
      kind="giant"
      label="White supergiant concept: pearl-white mottled photosphere and separated radial wind streaks persist through three flight compositions"
      moments={["01 / OUTFLOW", "02 / OUTFLOW", "03 / OUTFLOW"]}
    />
    <div class="reading">
      <p>
        <strong>Basis.</strong>
        The white-giant prompt becomes a specific A-type supergiant, inspired by
        Deneb: an evolved luminous star with a stellar wind. Observations
        support mass outflow and time-dependent ejections, rather than a large
        copy of the Sun.
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1007.2095"
          >Deneb and Rigel spectroscopy / interferometry</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pilot hypothesis.</strong>
        Abundant light tempts a sustained collection pass, but the outflow makes
        lingering costly. Enter obliquely, take energy and turn away. The
        distinctive choice is exposure to a persistent hostile environment,
        rather than waiting for an eruption.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Visible identity.</strong>
        A pearl-white photosphere, fine luminous mottling and disconnected
        outward streaks. Their persistence should say “this environment stays
        hostile”. The cool-looking colour must not imply safety.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      <strong>Arcade abstraction:</strong>
      visible wind strokes, exaggerated surface texture, compressed distances
      and survivable harvesting. Wind is plasma, not air resistance; no outward
      push or propulsion benefit follows from this sketch. It does not predict
      collection yield or damage.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="candidate" id="flare-dwarf">
    <p class="candidate-id">B / ACTIVE RED DWARF · OBSERVED NATURE</p>
    <h3>A dim ember with a violent temper</h3>
    <StellarCharacterSketch
      client:load
      kind="flare"
      label="Active red dwarf replacement concept: mottled red-orange surface, scattered bright arcs and an encompassing bloom that brightens and spreads, then subsides"
      moments={["01 / ACTIVE ATMOSPHERE", "02 / BROAD BLOOM", "03 / SUBSIDING"]}
    />
    <div class="reading">
      <p>
        <strong>Basis.</strong>
        Proxima Centauri is a red dwarf with observed powerful flares. Low
        steady luminosity does not make an active small star benign.
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/universe/exoplanets/neighboring-stars-bad-behavior-large-and-frequent-flares/"
          >NASA’s Proxima flare observations</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pilot hypothesis.</strong>
        Surface activity and a broad luminous atmosphere suggest greater
        exposure reach than the other encounters. Collection may still tempt an
        approach, but the pilot should expect to keep more distance. Whether
        activity also creates collection windows remains open.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Visible identity.</strong>
        Dark patches and orange cells distinguish the surface. Scattered arcs
        and an encompassing bloom carry that activity beyond the body when
        surface detail disappears at flight scale. This replacement tests a
        broad exposure cue; its exact texture and bloom are provisional.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      <strong>Arcade abstraction:</strong>
      visible circumstellar bloom and its relation to a larger gameplay hazard
      region. The storyboard shows increasing activity, not an accepted warning
      timer or a measured damage boundary. Bloom does not establish a safe edge.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="candidate" id="white-dwarf">
    <p class="candidate-id">C / DEBRIS-BEARING WHITE DWARF · OBSERVED NATURE</p>
    <h3>A hard light among the remains</h3>
    <StellarCharacterSketch
      client:load
      kind="ember"
      label="Debris-bearing white dwarf concept: compact white point with a hard cross of glare and a broken copper-coloured elliptical debris belt advancing around it"
      moments={["01 / FRAGMENTS", "02 / BELT ADVANCES", "03 / BELT ADVANCES"]}
    />
    <div class="reading">
      <p>
        <strong>Basis.</strong>
        A white dwarf is a dense stellar remnant, no longer powered by core
        fusion. Disrupted planetary material is observed around some white
        dwarfs; the debris belongs to this particular encounter, not the entire
        family.
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/resources/life-and-death/chapter-7/"
          >NASA on remnants</a
        >;
        <a
          href="https://astrobiology.nasa.gov/news/asteroid-remains-around-dead-stars-reveal-the-likely-fate-of-our-solar-system/"
          >observed planetary debris</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pilot hypothesis.</strong>
        A small luminous target sits within a moving obstacle belt. Collect on
        the near side, watch fragments crossing the exit, and resist diving
        toward the point. Compact appearance must not imply a weak gravitational
        commitment.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Visible identity.</strong>
        Almost featureless white glare contrasts with a broken, warm debris
        belt. The surroundings carry the character when the body is only a dot.
        A torn belt should read as material crossing a path, distinct from the
        Sun’s decorative rings.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      <strong>Arcade abstraction:</strong>
      individually visible, widely spaced fragments, traversable gaps and
      survivable near-remnant flight. The belt’s projection and drift are
      illustrative. No salvage reward, collision law, mass or collection reach
      is established.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="candidate" id="pulsar">
    <p class="candidate-id">D / PULSAR · OBSERVED REMNANT</p>
    <h3>A lighthouse with no safe side forever</h3>
    <StellarCharacterSketch
      client:load
      kind="pulsar"
      label="Pulsar concept: a tiny white-violet point and two opposed pale beams rotate from above the fixed Wasp, across it, then below it"
      moments={[
        "01 / SWEEP APPROACHES",
        "02 / BEAM CROSSES",
        "03 / SWEEP PASSES",
      ]}
    />
    <div class="reading">
      <p>
        <strong>Basis.</strong>
        A pulsar is a rotating neutron-star remnant whose radiation beams can
        sweep across an observer like a lighthouse. It is neither a blue Sun nor
        a periodically exploding star.
        <a href="https://www.nasa.gov/universe/stars/neutron-stars/pulsars/"
          >NASA on pulsars</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pilot hypothesis.</strong>
        Follow a rotating gap or cross just after the sweep; keeping one
        distance no longer answers the danger. The first opportunity could be
        passage through the encounter rather than collection. Ordinary solar
        collectors’ response to the emission remains an unresolved fiction
        choice.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Visible identity.</strong>
        An almost point-like white core, sparse violet structure and two
        continuous opposing beams. Rotation, rather than an expanding circular
        pulse, carries the warning. The central surface is deliberately not the
        main recognition cue.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">
      <strong>Arcade abstraction:</strong>
      normally invisible emission made visible, a three-dimensional beam mapped
      into the play plane, a greatly slowed sweep and survivable gaps. The
      sketch establishes no radius beyond which the beam is safe.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h3>The blue-dwarf prompt / a different kind of uncertainty</h3>
    <p>
      Stellar-evolution calculations predict that some red dwarfs eventually
      become hotter blue dwarfs, on timescales longer than the current age of
      the Universe. This is a predicted future state, not an observed
      present-day population, and “blue” does not mean cold.
      <a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/asna.200510440"
        >Adams, Bodenheimer and Laughlin’s evolution calculations</a
      >. It could belong in far-future fiction, but that basis alone supplies no
      distinctive surface activity or pilot decision. A blue-painted small Sun
      would not answer this study. Its encounter character remains unresolved;
      it is neither selected nor scientifically ruled out.
    </p>
    <h3>What this comparison can settle</h3>
    <p>
      These alternatives separate persistent outflow, broad stellar activity,
      moving material and a rotating sweep. Their colours support temperature
      and contrast; surface and surroundings carry the stronger distinctions.
      The saturated palettes, glare and exaggerated structure are graphic
      proposals, not naked-eye reconstructions of the objects.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plates make the proposed characters inspectable together. They do not
      establish that warnings are sufficient during a real approach, or that
      collection is worth the danger. A retained direction would next need its
      cue tested in a moving production flight composition with the actual HUD,
      ship, camera and competing effects. Field profiles and timing would follow
      that design evidence. No finding from the earlier spatial study selects
      any of these directions.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3 id="retained-direction">
      Retained direction / Light, reach and exposure must agree
    </h3>
    <p>
      Stellar variety changes how a star interacts with its surroundings, with
      visible identity communicating those differences. A colour substitution
      alone is insufficient. The standard Sun remains the established reference;
      its simple artwork does not limit the visual vocabulary of other natures.
      The comparison supports this direction, not acceptance of every depicted
      object or effect.
    </p>
    <p>
      The white supergiant’s abundant outward light suggests collection from
      farther away. The debris-bearing remnant suggests a smaller energy
      opportunity, while surface activity and atmospheric bloom suggest a larger
      exposure region for the active star. These relationships are intended game
      characters, not tuned profiles or measurements inferred from picture
      brightness. In particular, a hot white dwarf still radiates; compact size
      alone does not determine collector income. Exact yields and reaches remain
      open, and all cues must eventually agree with the actual fields.
    </p>
    <p>
      Surface differentiation and surrounding light are retained qualities for
      the active star. The local eruption drawing is replaced by the broad-bloom
      proposal above; the replacement’s appearance and event timing remain
      unaccepted. The pulsar remains optional. A vertical, bottom-to-top light
      axis with pulsing is a possible alternative to the rotating sweep, still
      requiring a concrete comparison. An intensity pulse on a fixed axis would
      be a deliberate arcade translation of pulsar emission, not the same
      phenomenon as a lighthouse beam crossing the observer.
    </p>
    <h3>One nature across World, FX and LFX</h3>
    <p>
      The encounter can use the three existing rendering layers together. A
      proposed division gives World the body, surface and physical surroundings;
      persistent FX the emitted light and fading aftermath; and LFX the current
      glare, bloom or active pulse. Their contributions should express the same
      stellar state. This does not require every star to use every layer, and
      fading history must not masquerade as a current hazard boundary. The
      plates remain single-canvas sketches; they do not demonstrate that
      composition in the production stack.
    </p>
    <p>
      The next focused comparison should test an accepted character relationship
      in the shared flight composition: whether distant collection, low yield or
      greater exposure is anticipated from the star while the real HUD and ship
      compete for attention. Exact artwork, field tuning and pulsar inclusion
      remain exploration choices before readiness and scope can be settled.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>03 / To be developed after exploration</h2>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

